# Reddit Ads Performance Report — anchor 2026-10-30

**Source:** Reddit Ads platform data (fixture), via platform performance checks and week-over-window comparisons. Media metrics only — platform-reported conversions are not qualified leads (per `/wiki/source-of-truth.md`).

## ⚠️ Data limitation first

The Reddit Ads platform tools in this environment return **account-level totals only** — they do not expose a per-campaign breakdown, and no per-campaign fixture data exists on disk. I will not invent campaign rows. What I *can* verify from the data and repo:

- **Only one Reddit Ads campaign is identified anywhere in the available sources:** **"Community boost"** (exact name per `/wiki/campaign-goals.md` and pending proposal `prop-c0b3b28e`, which explicitly attributes last-week spend of $775.25 to that campaign).
- The account-level totals are internally consistent with a single campaign of steady pacing: last-30d spend of $3,210.19 ≈ 4.1 weeks × ~$775/wk, and conversions outside the two reported weeks run ~6–7/wk as well (13 of 30d conversions fall in 2026-10-01 → 10-16, matching the weekly rate). No evidence of a hidden second campaign with divergent performance.

So the per-campaign tables below are effectively the account-level figures for "Community boost."

## (1) Last 30 days (2026-10-01 → 2026-10-30)

| Campaign | Spend | Conversions | CPA |
|---|---|---|---|
| Community boost (reddit_ads account total) | **$3,210.19** | **26** | **$123.47** |

## (2) Direction of travel — last week vs prior week

| Campaign | Metric | Prior week (10-17 → 10-23) | Last week (10-24 → 10-30) | Change |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Community boost | Spend | $752.84 | $775.25 | +$22.41 (+3.0%) |
| Community boost | Conversions | 6 | 7 | +1 (+16.7%) |
| Community boost | CPA | $125.47 | $110.75 | −$14.72 (−11.7%) |

## (3) Worst performer

**On the available evidence, Reddit Ads has no poor performer to flag — the opposite.** If "Community boost" is the only campaign (which the data is consistent with), it is trivially both the best and the worst campaign on the platform, and its trend is improving on every metric: spend nearly flat (+$22.41), conversions up 6 → 7, CPA down $14.72 from $125.47 to $110.75. The likely driver of the CPA improvement is flat spend with one extra conversion — not a structural change. Note that this $110.75 is the account's best platform-level CPA last week (Google Ads $191.18, Meta $190.00 per the 2026-10-24 → 10-30 weekly report), so Reddit's "worst campaign" is still the account's most efficient spend by CPA.

A worst-campaign call of "highest CPA with meaningful spend" or "zero conversions" or "worsening trend" **cannot be supported by this data** — none of those conditions hold at any level I can observe.

## (4) Caveats

- **No per-campaign split available** (fixture mode): campaign identification relies on the wiki and pending proposal `prop-c0b3b28e`, not on a platform per-campaign report. If the account actually runs multiple Reddit campaigns, a divergent laggard could be masked inside these totals.
- **Tiny conversion volume:** 6–7 conversions/week. A single conversion swings weekly CPA by roughly $16–18, so the −11.7% CPA move is directional, not statistically settled.
- **Data completeness:** data is complete through the anchor date 2026-10-30 for all three windows (last_30d = 10-01 → 10-30; last_week = 10-24 → 10-30; prior_week = 10-17 → 10-23). No truncation flag from the platform.
- **Conversions are platform-reported** and not comparable across platforms or to warehouse pipeline outcomes.

**Suggested next step:** if a per-campaign ranking is genuinely needed, pull the campaign-level report directly from Reddit Ads (or extend the fixture data) — the current tool surface cannot produce it, and I'd rather flag that gap than fill it with assumptions.

